  "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure is a clinical syndrome in which structural or functional heart problems impair the heart\u0027s ability to fill or pump blood effectively. It does not mean that the heart has stopped working. It is usually a long-term condition that needs ongoing management.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ETypes of Heart Failure\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EHeart failure is commonly classified by left ventricular ejection fraction:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHFrEF:\u003C/strong\u003E Reduced ejection fraction, 40% or less\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHFmrEF:\u003C/strong\u003E Mildly reduced ejection fraction, 41% to 49%\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EHFpEF:\u003C/strong\u003E Preserved ejection fraction, 50% or more\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ECauses and Symptoms\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003ECommon causes and contributing factors include coronary artery disease, hypertension, valve disease, cardiomyopathy, arrhythmias, diabetes, alcohol use and some cancer treatments. Typical symptoms include breathlessness, fatigue, reduced exercise tolerance, swelling, orthopnoea and rapid weight gain from fluid retention.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EDiagnosis and Treatment\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDiagnosis is based on symptoms and signs, supported by natriuretic peptide testing, ECG, echocardiography, blood tests and sometimes further imaging. Treatment depends on the type and cause of heart failure and may include medicines, devices, surgery, rehabilitation and lifestyle support.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Ch2\u003ERoles in the Care Team\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cul\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDoctors diagnose the condition, lead treatment and manage complications.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ENurses provide education, monitoring and coordination of care.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPharmacists review medicines and support safe use.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EDietitians provide individual nutrition advice.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EPhysiotherapists and exercise physiologists support safe physical activity.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003ESocial workers and psychologists help with practical and emotional challenges.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Ch2\u003EWorking Together\u003C/h2\u003E\u003Cp\u003EConsistent messages, accurate handovers and a shared care plan reduce confusion. Any team member who notices rapid weight gain, worsening breathlessness or new swelling should escalate their concerns according to local procedures. Severe symptoms require urgent medical assessment.\u003C/p\u003E"
}]]></Value>
